hint.txt:
    A C++ library (C++17), built with CMake (3.14 or later), with unit tests in GoogleTest and an installable CMake package.
    - Layout: the public header under include/{{NAME_SNAKE}}/ (include/{{NAME_SNAKE}}/{{NAME_SNAKE}}.hpp), the source under src/, everything in namespace {{NAME_SNAKE}}. Use {{NAME_SNAKE}} for C++ identifiers, the namespace, file names, include guards and CMake target names, and {{NAME}} for the CMake project and package name.
    - The placeholder library offers version(), returning the project version (for example "0.1.0"), and placeholder(), returning "{{NAME}}: placeholder - nothing implemented yet".
    - The library is static by default and shared with -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=ON. Give it an alias target {{NAME_SNAKE}}::{{NAME_SNAKE}}. Shared builds must work on macOS and Linux; Windows DLL exports aren't needed.
    - Install: cmake --install installs the headers, the library and a CMake package (with a version file, SameMajorVersion), so another project uses find_package({{NAME}}) and links {{NAME_SNAKE}}::{{NAME_SNAKE}}. It must also work without installing, from add_subdirectory or FetchContent, with the same target name.
    - Tests: an option {{NAME_SNAKE}}_BUILD_TESTS, on by default only when this is the top-level project, so a project that pulls it in with add_subdirectory or FetchContent doesn't need GoogleTest. When it's on, GoogleTest is required: use an installed copy from find_package(GTest), or download GoogleTest 1.15.2 with FetchContent (URL https://github.com/google/googletest/releases/download/v1.15.2/googletest-1.15.2.tar.gz, URL_HASH SHA256=7b42b4d6ed48810c5362c265a17faebe90dc2373c885e5216439d37927f02926) only when the developer opts in with {{NAME_SNAKE}}_FETCH_GOOGLETEST, off by default. With neither, configuring fails with a message saying how to install GoogleTest, opt in to the download, or turn the tests off with -D{{NAME_SNAKE}}_BUILD_TESTS=OFF. Register the tests with gtest_discover_tests.
    - tests/smoke.sh: in a temporary folder, configure and build with tests on, -D{{NAME_SNAKE}}_FETCH_GOOGLETEST=OFF and -DFETCHCONTENT_FULLY_DISCONNECTED=ON, run ctest, install into a temporary prefix, then build a small consumer program against the installed package with find_package({{NAME}}), run it, and check it prints the placeholder line and a version. Never download anything. Work with Apple clang on macOS and with gcc on Linux.
    - Warnings (-Wall -Wextra -pedantic) on for gcc and clang, without failing the build.
    - README: layout, building (static and shared), testing, installing and using it with find_package, add_subdirectory or FetchContent, how to get GoogleTest (Homebrew, apt, or the opt-in download), and the choices made.
    For an example of a C library with an installable package, read the c-lib type in the same catalog.
    GoogleTest is installed here, so --check-type builds and runs the tests.

